t"><p>Given an integer array <code>nums</code> and an integer <code>k</code=
>, return <em>the</em> <code>k</code> <em>most frequent elements</em>. You =
may return the answer in <strong>any order</strong>.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 1:</strong></p>

<div class=3D"example-block">
<p><strong>Input:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">nums =3D [1,1,1,2,2,3=
], k =3D 2</span></p>

<p><strong>Output:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">[1,2]</span></p>
</div>

<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 2:</strong></p>

<div class=3D"example-block">
<p><strong>Input:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">nums =3D [1], k =3D 1=
</span></p>

<p><strong>Output:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">[1]</span></p>
</div>

<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 3:</strong></p>

<div class=3D"example-block">
<p><strong>Input:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">nums =3D [1,2,1,2,1,2=
,3,1,3,2], k =3D 2</span></p>

<p><strong>Output:</strong> <span class=3D"example-io">[1,2]</span></p>
</div>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>

<ul>
	<li><code>1 &lt;=3D nums.length &lt;=3D 10<sup>5</sup></code></li>
	<li><code>-10<sup>4</sup> &lt;=3D nums[i] &lt;=3D 10<sup>4</sup></code></l=
i>
	<li><code>k</code> is in the range <code>[1, the number of unique elements=
 in the array]</code>.</li>
	<li>It is <strong>guaranteed</strong> that the answer is <strong>unique</s=
trong>.</li>
</ul>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Follow up:</strong> Your algorithm's time complexity must be bet=
ter than <code>O(n log n)</code>, where n is the array's size.</p>
